{"task":"Make room for the TUI branch: ANSWER, and a CA check that reads the config","goals":"Remove the two real incompatibilities between this work and claude/lucid-ember-7451","status":"Waiting","recorded_at":"2026-09-10T03:56:43Z","body":"Reviewing claude/lucid-ember-7451 against master turned up two places where the two lines of\nwork are not merely duplicated but actively incompatible. Both are fixed here, on master, so\nthat branch's merge does not have to carry them.\n\nThe first is a calling convention. That branch replaces the installer's prompts with an\narrow-key TUI, and its `ask` returns the answer in ANSWER rather than on stdout — because\nthe prompt is now DRAWN on stdout, so a `$(ask …)` capture swallows the prompt into the\nanswer, and a command substitution is a subshell, so nothing the function sets inside it\n(including how it ended) reaches the caller. Their commit describes what that combination\ndid: a validation loop spinning at 100% CPU printing a rejection tens of thousands of times,\nevery word of it swallowed by the capture.\n\nmaster had three `$(ask …)` call sites. Two predate both branches; the third is the\nPROXY_FALLBACK question added here two commits ago, so it is new breakage from this side.\nAll three now read ANSWER. `ask` still echoes as well, so nothing that captures it breaks\ntoday, and the comment says plainly which form to use and why.\n\nThe second is a factual claim doctor makes about certificates. That branch stops using\nCaddy's own CA and signs with mkcert's root instead, splicing a pki block into the rendered\nconfig — which means `caddy trust` is not needed at all, and mkcert is a dependency again.\ndoctor here asserted the opposite: it looked for \"Caddy Local Authority\" in the trust store\nand, when absent, prescribed `caddy trust`. Against a config signed by mkcert that reports on\na CA which signs none of these certificates and recommends a command that would not help.\n\nSo the check reads the config rather than assuming: a non-comment line naming rootCA.pem\nmeans the mkcert root, and the message and the fix follow from that.\n\nNon-comment matters, and getting it wrong is the regression the new test pins. The template's\nown prose explains why a wildcard certificate does not work and says \"mkcert\" while doing it,\nin a comment present in EVERY config including ones signing with Caddy's own CA. Matching on\nthe word alone reported the mkcert root for every install on the machine — which it did, once,\nbefore this was narrowed to the directive.\n\ntest/doctor-ca.test.sh covers all three states: a config using local_certs, one splicing a\npki block at mkcert's root, and one that merely mentions mkcert in a comment while using\nlocal_certs.\n\nNot changed here, and worth saying: the branches are closer than the overlap warnings\nsuggested. That branch merged master at acf343b and kept its logic, so it already carries the\nmode consolidation, the takedown, the port handling and the proxy work — its install.sh\nheader lists --proxy-fallback. What it does not yet have is 917b259, `ldev update`. The\ngenuinely unique work on that side is the TUI itself (lib/tui.sh, test/tui-menu.test.sh, the\n--plain and NO_COLOR degradation) and the mkcert signing change, neither of which exists here.\n","parent_commit":"917b2590cf979d24aaa18ef25563564218b2a2d4","repo":"brave-cedar-fb33","pct":"63","tasks":[{"id":"ask","content":"Convert every $(ask …) call site to read ANSWER, so the TUI branch's prompt drawing does not break them","status":"completed"},{"id":"ca","content":"doctor reads the config to decide which CA signs, instead of assuming Caddy's own","status":"completed"},{"id":"ca-comment","content":"Match the pki directive, not the template prose that mentions mkcert in every config","status":"completed"},{"id":"tests","content":"test/doctor-ca.test.sh — local_certs, mkcert pki block, and mkcert-in-a-comment","status":"completed","created_at":"2026-09-10T01:14:35.832Z","status_at":"2026-09-10T01:14:35.832Z"},{"id":"review","content":"Review claude/lucid-ember-7451 against master and report what is unique, duplicated and incompatible","status":"completed"},{"id":"merge-branches","content":"Merge claude/lucid-ember-7451 — it needs 917b259, and master needs its TUI and mkcert signing","status":"pending","human_reason":"The two branches are complementary, not rival: that branch merged master at acf343b and kept its logic, so it already carries the mode consolidation, takedown, port handling and proxy work. It lacks only 917b259 (ldev update); master lacks its TUI (lib/tui.sh, test/tui-menu.test.sh, --plain) and its mkcert signing. The two incompatibilities are fixed on master as of this commit, so the merge should now be mechanical — but landing another session's branch is Bryce's call, and that session may still be working in it."},{"id":"triaj-swap","content":"Retire sh.brew.caddy so ldev can own 443, now that triaj.ldev is a proxy site","status":"pending","human_reason":"Needs sudo and a terminal, which this session has neither of. triaj.ldev is now a proxy site pointing at the same 127.0.0.1:5273 the Homebrew caddy proxies to, so the swap no longer breaks it — but stopping sh.brew.caddy and installing com.ldev.caddy is root-owned and briefly takes triaj.ldev and matsu.ldev offline.","created_at":"2026-09-10T03:09:42.808Z","status_at":"2026-09-10T03:09:42.808Z"},{"id":"rehome-run","content":"Run ldev rehome against seasonal-drops","status":"pending","human_reason":"Rewrites live site data: 16,224 database rows plus WP_HOME/WP_SITEURL in wp-config.php. The command refuses to run without a terminal to confirm on. Do it after the swap, since the portless URL only works once the wildcard daemon owns 443.","created_at":"2026-09-09T19:22:30.481Z","status_at":"2026-09-09T19:22:30.481Z"}],"agent":"claude-code","changelog":"Unreviewed (agent unavailable).","code_quality":"Unreviewed (agent unavailable).","vulnerabilities":"Unreviewed (agent unavailabl